{
  "summary": "P1 credential compromise (KB-029). Aisha Khan (HR Generalist, user_id 5, aisha.khan@brightline.example, NY time) reports she clicked the link in a \"payroll verification\" email and typed her password into the site. Exact click time wasn't given; most likely shortly after a matching phish was received on 2026-10-10 ~20:56 UTC. Matching message in her quarantine: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain, capital I in place of l), subject \"Urgent: verify your payroll login\", marked phishing, not releasable. She may have gotten another copy in her inbox. Sign-in logs (14 days): 10 sign-ins, all successful, all from New York, US on known corporate IP 203.0.113.10 / residential IP 198.51.100.25, all within usual hours, all Duo push. No failures, no hosting-network IPs, nothing after 2026-10-09. No suspicious activity found, so block_sign_in was NOT requested. Duo enrolled (push, her phone). Actions: reset_password queued (approval id 1). Temp password to be given by phone after callback verification. Still needed: approve the reset and do callback; security review of the lookalike sender/domain and block it org-wide; check whether other users (especially HR/payroll) got the same phish; confirm the click time with the user and recheck sign-ins plus mailbox rules/forwarding for persistence; check whether she approved any Duo push she didn't start.",
  "reason": "KB-029 says always escalate; ticket is P1 credential compromise."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Aisha, thank you for telling us right away. Reporting it fast is the most helpful thing you could do. Since you typed your password into that page, we're treating it as stolen. Here's where things stand:\n\n1. I've asked for your password to be reset. Once a technician approves it, they'll call you at the number we have on file to confirm it's you, then give you a temporary password over the phone. You'll set a new password the next time you sign in. We'll never send a password by email.\n2. I checked your recent sign-ins and found nothing unusual. Everything came from your normal locations at your normal times. Our security team is also reviewing your case.\n3. Please don't delete the email, reply to it, or forward it. Leave it where it is so security can look at it.\n4. Never approve a Duo push you didn't start yourself. If you get an unexpected Duo prompt, deny it and contact us right away.\n5. If you remember roughly what time you clicked the link, please reply with it. That helps our investigation.\n\nThanks again for acting quickly.",
  "internal_note": "P1 phishing credential entry (KB-029, KB-008). User id 5, HR, not VIP, account active, Duo push enrolled. Matching phish in quarantine: Q-1003 from it-support@brightIine-help.example (lookalike domain), 'Urgent: verify your payroll login', received 2026-10-10 20:56 UTC, marked phishing and not releasable. Not released. Sign-in logs over 14 days: 10 successful sign-ins, all New York on known corporate/residential IPs, during usual hours, with Duo push. No hosting networks, no failures, nothing after 10-09. Because nothing looked suspicious, block_sign_in was not requested; reconsider if new activity shows up. reset_password queued (approval 1). Deliver temp password by phone after callback verification. Escalated to Tier 2/security (escalation 1) for sender block, a check of other recipients, mailbox rule/forwarding review, and confirming the click time.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-029",
    "KB-008"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
